      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000121707,
      "SummaryText": "FAR Research, Inc., dba FAR Chemical, Inc. (FAR) is a manufacturer of pharmaceutical intermediates and other fine organic chemicals. Bromine, trimethylchlorosilane (TMCS), dimethylamine (DMA) and titanium tetrachloride (TiCl4) are four chemicals used by FAR that are stored in sufficient quantity to require development and submission of a Risk Management Plan (RMP). This summary describes FAR's activities and safeguards which have been instituted to protect employees, the community, and the environment.\n\nFAR has operated at the Palm Bay, Florida facility since 1983. FAR synthesizes pharmaceutical intermediates and fine organic chemicals. These chemicals are sold to the pharmaceutical, cosmetic, electronics, and general chemical industries. Over the years, FAR's products have been used in compounds designed to fight Aids, Hepatitis B, and other diseases. Other FAR products have been used to facilitate the manufacture of electronic chips; others have been used by the space industry. One FAR product has improved the efficiency of the manufacture of nylon. FAR and its employees are committed to continue to safely manufacture organic compounds that make a positive difference.\n\nFAR\u0092s greatest strength is its employees.  The technical, operations, and support groups are committed to the safe development and manufacture of the products synthesized. The typical \"path\" for a new product at FAR begins with a customer need, followed by a thorough literature search for safety and process information. Upon completion of the background work, a synthesis route is proposed and the laboratory work commences. Chemists and technicians complete process development on a small, glassware scale to ensure that the synthesis can be completed safely. The next step includes the writing of a proposed, detailed procedure to be completed at the pilot scale. A process safety review meeting is then scheduled. The review participants include the process development chemist, the Operations Director, t"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000121707,
      "SummaryText": "he Director of R&D, the EHS Manager and the VP of Business Development. Other staff may be involved if warranted. The review includes verification that proper development procedures have been followed and that available safety and health information has been obtained, checked, and included with the batch record. Any recommended changes made by the committee must be completed prior to scale-up of the process. The review committee may also require that special operator training sessions be held prior to running certain compounds or chemistries. If not completed before, prior to the project moving from pilot scale to production a detailed process hazard analysis (PHA) will be completed and recommendations addressed.\n\nProcess equipment at FAR is designed for its intended use. Process vessels are hastelloy, glass lined or grade 316 stainless steel. The vessels are ASME Code rated and are protected with rupture discs and/or relief valves. If corrosion is of concern, testing may be completed prior to running in a given vessel. Glass lined vessels may be spark tested to verify integrity. Alloy vessels are tested using ultrasonic wall thickness testing devices. Key vessels and equipment are checked by maintenance at least annually.  Both the TMCS and the bromine tanks are within containment dikes designed to hold more than the maximum volume of each tank.  In addition, the TMCS tank is protected with an AFFF 3% medium expansion foam deluge system.\n\nThe two processes using DMA & TiCl4 occur in campaigns and there is no permanent storage of DMA & TiCl4.  These two chemicals are delivered in pressure rated tank containers (isotainers).  The DMA is stored in the isotainer and fed directly into the manufacturing process.  The TiCl4 will be stored in the isotainer and repackaged into cylinders for sale to customers.  When the campaign is completed the empty isotainers are sent back to the supplier.    The intent is to only receive enough material to complete the scheduled campaign"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000121707,
      "SummaryText": ".  The amount of DMA will be received in amounts ranging from 12,000 to 25,000 pounds.  The amount of TiCl4 received may be as much as 55,000 pounds.  While the TiCl4 is onsite the isotainer is kept within secondary containment.  The DMA is a gas so secondary containment is not required.\n\nLiquefied bromine is stored in a 2,000-gallon storage tank and a 1,500-gallon receiving vessel/reactor. TMCS is stored in a 5,000-gallon pressure rated carbon steel tank. The maximum quantities of bromine and TMCS inventoried are 56,000 pounds and 35,000 pounds, respectively. FAR's policy is to minimize inventory of all raw materials. The materials are delivered by bulk tanker in quantities of approximately 30,000 pounds. Orders are not placed unless inventories are less than 15,000 pounds for bromine and 2,000 pounds for TMCS. Bromine inventory is verified by tank load cells, receiving and usage records.  TMCS inventory is verified by level gauge, receiving and usage records.\n\nNo accidental releases of the regulated chemicals have occurred in the past five years.\n\nFAR continues to improve its equipment, processes and procedures with a continued focus on safety for both our employees, community and the environment.   We continue to specialize in finding solutions to our customers\u0092 needs."
    }
